/! What I Learned					
section
	.container
		.row
			.col-lg-12
				h2 What I learned
				p.lead.mb-3 Working on United Way of Central Maryland reinforced how important the relationship between design and development is to creating a successful digital experience. Joining a project after the initial implementation encountered challenges required me to understand the existing work, identify what wasn't functioning as intended, and determine how to move forward while respecting the approved designs and project goals.
				p.lead The experience strengthened my ability to troubleshoot complex WordPress implementations, balance design fidelity with technical requirements, and think critically about how website structure, performance, and functionality influence the user experience. It also reinforced the importance of building flexible solutions that support the people responsible for maintaining and updating the website. Supporting the transition to United Way's updated national branding gave me another opportunity to work at the intersection of visual design, development, and design systems. Creating the Figma style guide showed how documenting standards can help bridge the gap between design and implementation, improve consistency, and make future collaboration more efficient.
				p.lead Most importantly, the project reinforced that delivering a successful website involves more than reproducing a design. It requires understanding the organization's goals, anticipating user and content needs, solving technical challenges, and creating an experience that can continue evolving after launch.
